§5 WIRED — THE PIPELINE REACHES 'ready'. 2026-08-04T05:34:46Z

Until this landed, NO code path could set status='ready' and no writer existed for
user_sections, user_section_embeddings or user_section_anchors. Every stage was built and
tested in isolation and none was connected: an uploaded document was parsed and stopped.

Now: parse -> chunk -> anchor -> embed -> store -> ready, in one visible status walk.
'ready' is set in exactly ONE place, AFTER the transactional write.

== PASS (real .docx, real chunker, real KJV index, real DeepInfra call, real DB) ==
  ✓ test/user-corpus/pipeline-to-ready.test.ts > a document goes in and comes out searchable > reaches ready, with sections, vectors and anchors all written 2339ms
  ✓ test/user-corpus/pipeline-to-ready.test.ts > a document goes in and comes out searchable > wrote a model_slug the CORPUS will actually join against
  ✓ test/user-corpus/pipeline-to-ready.test.ts > a document goes in and comes out searchable > found BOTH anchor channels, and their agreement survives
  ✓ test/user-corpus/pipeline-to-ready.test.ts > a document goes in and comes out searchable > re-indexing is idempotent — a retry replaces rather than duplicates 1254ms
  Tests  4 passed (4)

What the assertions cover:
  * status reaches 'ready' (the assertion this whole slice worked toward)
  * embeddings == sections — a section without a vector is FTS-searchable and invisible to
    semantic search: half-indexed, and reporting ready
  * anchors > 0, with BOTH channels present and at least one verse found by both — the pair
    migration 103 put 'channel' in the PK to preserve
  * the stored model_slug joins against the corpus's OWN recorded value, read from the
    database (user 'bge-large-en-v1.5' vs corpus 'BAAI/bge-large-en-v1.5', normalised)
  * re-indexing is idempotent

== RED-PROOFS ==
SEED 1 - drop the DELETE from storeSections (every retry doubles the index): 4 failed
SEED 2 - set 'ready' BEFORE storing, i.e. the false claim of searchability: 3 failed

== UNVERIFIED, AND IT IS THE A6 SHAPE ==
bible-index.ts reads web/public/bible/kjv with fs at runtime. Locally that is a symlink and
works. ON VERCEL IT IS NOT VERIFIED: 'vercel --prod' uploads web/ ALONE and Next traces only
the files it can see, which is exactly how three A6 deploys died. public/ ships as static
assets; whether a serverless function can fs.readFileSync them is a separate question this
slice has NOT answered. indexHealth() exists so the answer is a check rather than a surprise,
and step 7's browser DoD is where it gets exercised for real.
